From 53638e3f239a203fcb4b4bdafee07b3290e0e031 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 04:38:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(desktop): a dead runtime's prompt no longer keeps a chat
 red; every red row reports why [daveey]
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

selfheal desktop-54ae373894 (t_01a1d3142a47913f). Session 20261111_060430_751449 sat with a red `stuck`
stripe at 06:54 with no blocked card, no review wait and a `done` outcome, and its agent, asked "why are you
red", blamed a scheduled card.

Problem: red stripe on a finished chat with no open question or blocked card.
Why 1: the dot was `needs-input` — $attentionSessionIds still held the stored session.
Why 2: runtime 66877047 kept `needsInput` from a clarify (06:15:34) open when the hub backend died (06:32:02,
  turn interrupted); startup resume auto-continued the session on a NEW runtime c442506d whose turn finished.
Why 3: nothing retires a prompt on a runtime the backend replaced: reconnect keeps `needsInput` on purpose
  (same-backend blip), only the active gateway's active_list poll reaps vanished runtimes, and evictable()
  keeps needsInput states. Reproduced: session-states-stale-prompt.test.ts, 3 of 8 cases fail on the base.
Root (happened): no rule that a turn on runtime R of stored session S proves S's other, non-busy prompt
  runtimes dead.
Root (not caught): the red verdict lived only in the renderer; desktop.log skipped first sightings and named
  no inputs, and no tool showed the agent its own stripe's reasons, so it guessed.

Countermeasures:
- symptom/root: publishSessionState retires the prompt of every other non-busy runtime of the same stored
  session once one runtime runs a turn (busy/turnLive) — session-states-superseded-prompts.ts.
- detection: stuckReasons() names each red input (needs-input:prompt runtime=<ids> | paused-queue | unknown,
  stalled, kanban-blocked:<n> on=<cards> held= asks=, review:<pr>, outcome:decision). Every red edge (first
  sighting, cause change, way out) goes into the stripe-transition log line and is POSTed to the session's
  own gateway (POST /api/sessions/{id}/stripe-report), which logs it and keeps it in
  <home>/logs/desktop-stripe.jsonl (2 MiB, one rotation). `hermes sessions outcome <id>` (and --json
  `desktop_stripe`) prints the latest report for the chat's lineage.

Proof: vitest src/store/session-states-stale-prompt.test.ts red on base (3 failed), green after; 70 desktop
store/sidebar/outcome files (890 tests) pass; tsc -p . clean; pytest test_session_outcome_surfaces.py +
test_serve_handler_ledger.py pass.
